WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.760
Through the power of computer&nbsp;
graphics research, today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.600
we can write wondrous programs which can simulate&nbsp;
all kinds of interactions in virtual worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.440
These works are some of my favorites, and&nbsp;
looking at the results, one would think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.120
these algorithms are so advanced, there is&nbsp;
hardly anything new to invent in this area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.400
But as amazing as these previous techniques&nbsp;
are, they don’t come without limitations.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.440
Alright, well, what do those limitations look&nbsp;
like? Let’s have a look at this example. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:45.840
water is coming out of the nozzle, and it behaves&nbsp;
unnaturally. But that’s only the smaller problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.200
there is an even bigger one. What is that&nbsp;
problem? Let’s slow this down, and look carefully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:02.000
Oh! Where did the water go? Yes, this is&nbsp;
the classical numerical dissipation problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.160
in fluid simulations, where due to an averaging&nbsp;
step, particles disappear into thin air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.360
And now, hold on to your papers and let’s see if&nbsp;
this new method can properly address this problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.760
And…oh yes! Fantastic!

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:28.560
So it dissipates less. Great! What else does this&nbsp;
do? Let’s have a look through this experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.680
where a wheel gets submerged into sand, that’s&nbsp;
good, but…the simulation is a little mellow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.840
You see, the sand particles&nbsp;
are flowing down like a fluid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:48.000
and the wheel does not really roll up the&nbsp;
particles in the air. And the new one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.200
So, apparently, it not only&nbsp;
helps with numerical dissipation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.400
but also with particle separation&nbsp;
too. More value. I like it.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.560
If this technique can really solve these two&nbsp;
phenomena, we don’t even need sandy tires and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.240
water sprinklers to make it shine, there are&nbsp;
so many scenarios where it performs better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:18.080
than previous techniques. For instance, when&nbsp;
simulating this non-frictional elastic plate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:25.520
with previous methods, some of the particles get&nbsp;
glued to it. And, did you catch the other issue?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.040
Yes, the rest of the particles also&nbsp;
refuse to slide off of each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:40.400
And now, let’s see the new method. Oh my! It&nbsp;
can simulate these phenomena correctly too!

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.360
And it does not stop there. It also simulates&nbsp;
strand-strand interactions better than previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.360
methods. In these cases, sometimes the&nbsp;
collision of short strands with boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.680
was also simulated incorrectly. Look at how all&nbsp;
this geometry intersected through the brush.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.960
And the new method? Yes, of course,&nbsp;
it addresses these issues too.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.000
So, if it can simulate the movement and&nbsp;
intersection of short strands better…does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:22.960
that mean that it can also perform higher-quality&nbsp;
hair simulations? Oh yes, yes it does! Excellent!

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.760
So, as you see, the pace of progress&nbsp;
in computer graphics research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.840
is absolutely stunning. Things&nbsp;
that were previously impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.400
can become possible in a matter of just&nbsp;
one paper. What a time to be alive!

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

